148
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:35,280
pleasurable experience for yourself and others.

149
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:41,200
It comes as no surprise, therefore, that conversation skills are one of the most

150
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:45,480
basic skills needed to function well in everyday society.

151
00:14:46,040 --> 00:14:51,360
One of the most important conversation skills for you to have is the ability to

152
00:14:51,360 --> 00:14:54,920
avoid using offensive or hurtful language.

153
00:14:54,920 --> 00:15:02,040
This becomes especially difficult when you are not aware that the words you use are

154
00:15:02,040 --> 00:15:03,840
actually considered hurtful.

155
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:09,960
Slang words commonly used by the younger generation can also be unknowingly

156
00:15:09,960 --> 00:15:17,000
offensive. To assist with this confusion, here are a few common words or phrases

157
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,600
which you may or may not know are actually hurtful.

158
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:24,340
The first one is I don't care.

159
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:31,360
This is a very common phrase for someone to say in a variety of situations, and it

160
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:33,880
is obvious that it can easily offend.

161
00:15:35,100 --> 00:15:41,500
It is never nice to say that you don't care about something or someone, even in a

162
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:47,660
joking manner. To those who are a bit more sensitive, they might take your words

163
00:15:47,660 --> 00:15:49,720
more seriously than you think.

164
00:15:50,960 --> 00:15:56,680
Altogether, it is best to assume that this phrase is offensive in most situations.

165
00:15:57,860 --> 00:16:00,940
The second is it was just a joke.

166
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:07,700
If a person has trouble tolerating whatever you just said, they won't believe you

167
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:08,780
say you weren't serious.

168
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:15,880
No matter how close you are as friends or family, if you make an offensive comment

169
00:16:15,880 --> 00:16:21,420
and expect them to take it as a joke, it might be a little too much to ask.

170
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:26,880
Although you may have had good intentions, it always pays to be careful about what

171
00:16:26,880 --> 00:16:32,260
you say out loud, especially if you know the person is a little bit sensitive.

172
00:16:33,540 --> 00:16:37,180
third is never mind, you don't get it.

173
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:43,740
Nobody likes being left out of the loop, and used in the wrong context, this can

174
00:16:43,740 --> 00:16:47,760
make it sound like you left the person out on purpose.

175
00:16:48,620 --> 00:16:53,120
Although you may have only meant for it to be a quick dismissal of a subject, it

176
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:58,580
could come off as rude, which destroys relationships rather than maintains them.

177
00:16:59,340 --> 00:17:04,140
Next time, try providing an explanation, no matter how brief.

178
00:17:05,180 --> 00:17:07,900
The fourth is what's the point?

179
00:17:07,900 --> 00:17:14,120
Although you may have asked this from a viewpoint of curiosity, it is easy for this

180
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:16,060
statement to sound offensive.

181
00:17:17,360 --> 00:17:22,840
Depending on the sensitivity or the person and the circumstance, it might sound like

182
00:17:22,840 --> 00:17:27,360
you are dismissing their explanation before they even try explaining.

183
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:34,080
It sounds rude, and there are many more polite ways to ask about the purpose of an

184
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:39,700
